Z||000005||mshill||12-03-2000||04:20 PM||||Rockcrawl is right.  All I am really concerened about is when the California DMV doesn't call it a Fiero anymore.  Since my F50 project is not going to be stretched and will have what looks like a stock 2.8L (really a 3.4L dressed down) the DMV won't have a problem with it.  However,  my next project (likely to be a Diablo) with a V8, they may think differntly.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||12-03-2000||07:02 PM||||In most states it's whatever the VIN says it is. Unless you do major frame alterations most state would consider any rebody & motor setup to still be Fiero.<P>If you do allot of frame mods some states may require special inspections to pass before they renew the registration. NJ does this to allot of suspension altered vehicles. (Mostly lifted trucks but I hear it's comming to lowered cars too.)||||reg||1
Z||000007||2M4 Dale||12-04-2000||09:27 AM||||I would have to say that as long as that Fiero space frame resides under the body panels somewhere then it's still a Fiero.<P>Fieros are great looking cars !!!! But some of the kits out there are awesome !!!! As well as some of the engine transplants !!!!||||reg||6
